{"version":1,"title":"Reject Handling SOP Finalization and QC Work Instruction","backing_conversation_id":"6ad1dc4d-24d0-83ed-829e-21c71382e865","widget_session_id":"0d25cd54-6326-4155-947c-817c6f837e45","widget_state":{"status":"completed","is_static_preview":true,"report_message":{"id":"354a81b4-30dd-4d99-9f98-d01a544a42aa","author":{"role":"assistant","name":null,"metadata":{"real_author":"tool:web"}},"create_time":1792138937.959391,"update_time":null,"content":{"content_type":"text","parts":["# Reject Handling SOP Finalization and QC Work Instruction\n\n## Executive summary\n\nThe existing **Reject Handling and Destruction Procedure** already establishes two different production-reject paths: rejects **below 2.5%** are handled through routine daily reporting and QC random verification, while rejects **exceeding 2.5%** require a dedicated reject/disposal report and management approval before physical disposal. The same document applies a similar below/above-2.5% distinction to OBM warehouse returns. Because the wording is strictly “less than 2.5%” and “exceeding 2.5%,” the case of **exactly 2.5% is not defined** in the existing procedure. fileciteturn0file0\n\nThe final draft preserves that threshold logic but adds the controls requested here: routine Assembly, Injection and Packaging rejects below 2.5% use **F-7-5-3-12-2, Daily Production & Rejection Record**, with no F-7-5-7-8 rejection slip required; Quarantine rejects require the Inspection Report, a representative rejected sample, F-7-5-7-8, and Quality Manager/Factory Director signatures; every OBM reject now receives F-7-5-7-8; supplier-related rejects require Purchasing Manager involvement to assess Return-to-Vendor feasibility; damaged product receives **HOLD QC** status; and formal disposition branches are Rework, Return to Vendor, Transfer to Warehouse for Destruction, or Other.\n\nOne structural point should be kept clear in document control: **F-7-5-7-8 is the form titled “Rejection Slip for Material / Products / Parts”; it is not the SOP document number.** The supplied SOP identifies itself as **Doc. No. REJ**. I therefore retained **REJ** for the SOP and referenced F-7-5-7-8 as its controlled rejection record. fileciteturn0file0\n\nFor standards alignment, **ISO 13485:2016 remains the current medical-device QMS standard and was confirmed again in 2025**. It is the stronger primary reference for this procedure because ISO describes it as specifically addressing medical-device quality systems, regulatory requirements, risk and supply-chain controls. citeturn2view1turn5search16 ISO 9001 has just changed: **ISO 9001:2026 was published on 16 September 2026 and replaced ISO 9001:2015**, so the drafts reference the 2026 edition as a supplemental generic QMS standard rather than treating the now-withdrawn 2015 edition as current. citeturn1search0turn1search4\n\nThe Saudi regulatory context also strongly supports using ISO 13485 as the principal framework. SFDA lists the Saudi/GSO adoption of ISO 13485 and publishes **MDS-G024, Guidance for ISO 13485 Requirements and Corresponding SFDA-MDS Requirements**, with both English and Arabic official pages. citeturn7search1turn9search1turn9search6\n\n## Finalized policy design\n\n### Mandatory wording incorporated into the SOP\n\nFor **Quarantine rejects**, the operative wording has been finalized as:\n\n> **When a reject is received from Quarantine, QC shall be provided with the applicable Inspection Report and a representative rejected sample. If the sample cannot physically accompany the documents, it shall be uniquely identified and available for review. QC shall prepare F-7-5-7-8 “Rejection Slip for Material / Products / Parts”. The rejection package shall be reviewed and signed by the Quality Manager and Factory Director before final disposition.**\n\nThis creates a complete evidence chain: inspection result → physical/traceable sample → rejection slip → management decision → disposition evidence. That is materially stronger than a reject slip with only a written reason because the disposition decision remains tied to objective inspection evidence and the actual defect.\n\nFor **OBM rejects**, the new rule is deliberately stronger than the existing SOP. The current SOP only requires random verification and a Focus-system entry for OBM rejects below 2.5%, while a separate report is required when the OBM reject rate exceeds 2.5%. fileciteturn0file0 The finalized revision states:\n\n> **For every OBM reject, QC shall prepare F-7-5-7-8 and obtain the signatures of the Quality Manager and Factory Director. The existing 2.5% threshold shall continue to determine the level of escalation: below 2.5%, QC random verification and the Focus-system entry remain applicable; above 2.5%, Area Supervisor approval is additionally required. Rejected products approved for destruction shall then be returned to the Warehouse for destruction, and destruction evidence shall be returned to QC for record closure.**\n\nThere is a small logical overlap in the requested rules: one instruction says OBM rejects are returned to Warehouse for destruction, while another asks for management-selectable **Rework / Return to Vendor / Other** branches. The documents reconcile those requirements by making warehouse destruction the normal OBM destruction route, while permitting the **Quality Manager and Factory Director to explicitly select an alternative disposition** on F-7-5-7-8. That avoids creating a contradictory SOP in which management is given disposition authority but OBM disposition is already predetermined.\n\nFor a **supplier-supplied rejected product**, the final wording is:\n\n> **When the rejected material, product, or part is supplied by an external supplier, the Purchasing Manager shall be contacted before irreversible disposition. Purchasing shall assess and document whether Return to Vendor is commercially and operationally feasible. The final disposition for cases requiring F-7-5-7-8 shall be selected and approved by the Quality Manager and Factory Director.**\n\nPurchasing is therefore a required **consulted/coordinating function**, not the final quality-disposition authority. This separation is preferable because supplier logistics and commercial return feasibility belong with Purchasing, while product-quality disposition remains a quality/management decision. ISO 13485 specifically applies not only to manufacturers but also to suppliers and external parties involved in the medical-device supply chain. citeturn2view1turn5search8\n\nFor routine **Assembly, Injection and Packaging** rejects, the rule is now explicit:\n\n> **Daily rejects generated from Assembly, Injection, and Packaging are considered normal production rejects when the daily reject rate is below 2.5%. F-7-5-7-8 is not required for these routine below-threshold rejects. The rejects shall be recorded in F-7-5-3-12-2 “Daily Production & Rejection Record”, and QC Compliance shall perform random physical verification.**\n\nThis keeps the SOP from generating unnecessary individual rejection reports for ordinary daily scrap while retaining controlled production data and QC verification.\n\nFor **damaged product**, the status-control requirement is:\n\n> **If a product is damaged, a clearly visible tag marked “HOLD QC” shall be attached immediately. The product shall remain segregated under HOLD QC until the applicable verification and disposition decision are complete.**\n\nThis status identification is consistent with ISO 13485's emphasis on product identification, traceability, and control of nonconforming product. ISO states that ISO 13485 is intended to support consistent, regulatory-compliant production and delivery of medical devices and places greater emphasis than generic ISO 9001 on regulatory requirements and risk management. citeturn2view1turn11search13\n\n### Threshold rule retained without inventing a 2.5% answer\n\nThe existing procedure does **not** say “2.5% or more”; it separately says **less than 2.5%** and **exceeding 2.5%**. Therefore, treating exactly 2.5% as automatically critical would be a policy change rather than a faithful interpretation. fileciteturn0file0\n\nThe final draft consequently says:\n\n> **Exactly 2.5%: handling is currently unspecified. QC shall not automatically classify the case as below-threshold or above-threshold. Until the policy gap is formally resolved, the affected product shall remain under HOLD QC and documented direction shall be obtained from the Quality Manager before disposition.**\n\nThat is an interim containment mechanism, not a redefinition of the threshold.\n\n## Decision flow and action timeline\n\nThe finalized process has one common entry point—identify, segregate and status-control the reject—followed by source-specific documentation and a controlled disposition decision.\n\n![Reject Handling Decision Flow](sandbox:/mnt/data/Reject_Handling_Decision_Flow.png)\n\nThe same logic is provided in Mermaid form for use in controlled procedures, presentations, or QMS documentation:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[Reject identified] --> B{Product damaged?}\n\n    B -- Yes --> C[Attach HOLD QC tag]\n    B -- No --> D{Identify source}\n    C --> D\n\n    D -- Daily Production --> E{Reject rate}\n    E -- Below 2.5% --> F[F-7-5-3-12-2 only<br/>QC random verification<br/>No F-7-5-7-8]\n    E -- Exactly 2.5% --> G[Unspecified<br/>Maintain HOLD QC<br/>Escalate to Quality Manager]\n    E -- Above 2.5% --> H[Prepare F-7-5-7-8<br/>Area Supervisor + Quality Manager + Factory Director]\n\n    D -- Quarantine --> I[Inspection Report + representative sample<br/>Prepare F-7-5-7-8<br/>Quality Manager + Factory Director]\n    D -- OBM --> J[Prepare F-7-5-7-8 for every OBM reject<br/>Retain threshold-specific controls]\n    D -- Supplier Product --> K[Contact Purchasing Manager<br/>Assess Return-to-Vendor feasibility]\n\n    I --> K2{Supplier supplied?}\n    K2 -- Yes --> K\n    K2 -- No --> L\n\n    H --> L{Disposition selected by<br/>Quality Manager + Factory Director}\n    J --> L\n    K --> L\n\n    L --> M[Rework]\n    L --> N[Return to Vendor]\n    L --> O[Transfer to Warehouse<br/>for Destruction]\n    L --> P[Other - describe]\n\n    M --> Q[QC reinspection / closure evidence]\n    N --> R[Purchasing coordination / return evidence]\n    O --> S[Warehouse destruction / completion evidence]\n    P --> T[Documented controlled action]\n\n    F --> U[Close record]\n    G --> U\n    Q --> U\n    R --> U\n    S --> U\n    T --> U\n```\n\nThe supplied company SOP does not specify hours or working-day limits, so any timeline inserted as a mandatory SLA would be newly created company policy rather than an existing requirement. The documents therefore identify the following as **recommended operating targets**, not as already-approved limits:\n\n| Target time | Action | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| Immediately / within 1 hour | Segregate the rejected product; apply HOLD QC if damaged; prevent unintended use or movement. | Proposed target |\n| Same shift / within 4 hours | QC verifies item, lot, quantity and defect; Quarantine provides Inspection Report and sample. | Proposed target |\n| By end of the same working day | Prepare F-7-5-7-8 where required; complete applicable OBM Focus entry. | Proposed target |\n| Within 1 working day | Obtain management disposition/signatures. | Proposed target |\n| Within 1 working day of supplier notification | Purchasing assesses Return-to-Vendor feasibility. | Proposed target |\n| Within 1 working day after approval | Execute rework transfer, supplier-return coordination, or Warehouse handover. | Proposed target |\n| Within 3 working days after destruction approval | Warehouse completes destruction or documents its scheduled destruction date and provides closure evidence. | Proposed target |\n\nDocumenting records, traceability and controlled handling is especially important in the medical-device context. ISO 13485 remains the current internationally recognized medical-device QMS standard, and ISO's own implementation material describes it as covering organizations across medical-device production and supply-chain activities. citeturn11search13turn5search1\n\n## Approval and disposition controls\n\n### Approval and signature matrix\n\nThe matrix below is the clearest way to prevent inconsistent signatures between QC, Production, Warehouse and management.\n\n| Reject case | Controlled record | Verification / consultation | Required approval |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Daily Production **<2.5%** | F-7-5-3-12-2 | QC random verification | No F-7-5-7-8 management signatures |\n| Daily Production **>2.5%** | F-7-5-7-8 | Area Supervisor | Area Supervisor + Quality Manager + Factory Director |\n| Daily Production **=2.5%** | Threshold handling unspecified | QC holds material; Quality Manager gives documented interim direction | According to interim written direction |\n| Quarantine reject | Inspection Report + sample + F-7-5-7-8 | QC verifies inspection/sample package | Quality Manager + Factory Director |\n| Quarantine supplier reject | Same as above | Purchasing Manager assesses Return to Vendor | Quality Manager + Factory Director |\n| OBM **<2.5%** | F-7-5-7-8 + OBM findings + Focus entry | QC random verification | Quality Manager + Factory Director |\n| OBM **>2.5%** | F-7-5-7-8 + OBM findings | Area Supervisor | Area Supervisor + Quality Manager + Factory Director |\n| Return to Vendor | F-7-5-7-8 where applicable | Purchasing Manager coordinates return | Quality Manager + Factory Director |\n| Warehouse destruction | Signed F-7-5-7-8 plus applicable threshold approvals | Warehouse receives matching item/lot/quantity | Previously approved disposition; Warehouse executes only |\n\nThe change from **Factory Manager** in the existing procedure to **Factory Director** in the final draft is deliberate because the requested approval role and the supplied F-7-5-7-8 sample use Factory Director. The existing SOP currently names Factory Manager as the final authorization role. fileciteturn0file0 Document Control should therefore standardize the title throughout the QMS rather than leave both titles active unless they genuinely refer to different positions.\n\n### Disposition comparison\n\nFor every case requiring F-7-5-7-8, the **Quality Manager and Factory Director jointly select the disposition**.\n\n| Disposition | Appropriate use | Signatories / involvement | Evidence required to close |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Rework** | Product can be corrected under a controlled method. | Quality Manager + Factory Director | Approved rework instruction and QC reinspection result |\n| **Return to Vendor** | Supplier-related reject and Purchasing confirms return is possible. | Quality Manager + Factory Director; Purchasing Manager coordinates | Supplier/return authorization and return/shipping evidence |\n| **Transfer to Warehouse for Destruction** | Product will not be reworked or returned. | Quality Manager + Factory Director; Area Supervisor additionally when the >2.5% rule applies | Warehouse handover and destruction completion evidence |\n| **Other** | Another controlled disposition is justified. | Quality Manager + Factory Director | Exact action, responsible function, destination and closure evidence must be written |\n\nFor **Rework**, the medical-device standard deserves particular attention: ISO 13485 contains a specific rework provision under its nonconforming-product controls, which is one reason the draft requires controlled rework instructions followed by QC reinspection rather than merely checking “Rework” on the form. SFDA's MDS-G024 is specifically intended to map ISO 13485 requirements to Saudi medical-device requirements. citeturn9search1turn11search13\n\n## ISO and Saudi regulatory basis\n\n### ISO 13485 as the primary standard\n\nAs of October 2026, **ISO 13485:2016 remains published/current and was confirmed in 2025**. ISO identifies it as the internationally recognized QMS standard for medical-device design and manufacture and notes its stronger focus on regulatory requirements and risk than generic ISO 9001. citeturn2view1turn5search16\n\nThe relevant clause structure for this reject-handling SOP is:\n\n| ISO 13485:2016 area | Practical relevance to this SOP |\n|---|---|\n| **Clause 4.2.5 — Control of records** | Supports retention of the rejection slip, daily records, inspection evidence, signatures, supplier-return evidence and destruction closure records. |\n| **Clause 7.4 — Purchasing** | Supports formal control of externally supplied product and the Purchasing Manager's involvement when rejected supplier product may be returned. |\n| **Clause 7.5.8 — Identification** | Supports clear reject-status identification and use of HOLD QC to distinguish material that must not be used or moved without authorization. |\n| **Clause 7.5.9 — Traceability** | Supports item code, lot/batch, quantity, transfer and disposition traceability. |\n| **Clause 8.3.1 — General control of nonconforming product** | Supports a documented process, clear responsibilities and controls preventing unintended use of rejects. |\n| **Clause 8.3.2 — Nonconforming product detected before delivery** | Supports controlled disposition of rejects identified internally before release. |\n| **Clause 8.3.3 — Nonconforming product detected after delivery** | Supports appropriate action and records when a problem is discovered after distribution/delivery. |\n| **Clause 8.3.4 — Rework** | Supports documented and controlled rework followed by appropriate verification before product is accepted. |\n\nThese are paraphrased implementation summaries rather than reproduced ISO text. SFDA's official MDS-G024 guidance maps this ISO 13485 clause structure to Saudi medical-device requirements, including records, purchasing, identification, traceability and the 8.3 nonconforming-product family. citeturn9search1turn9search6\n\nSFDA also maintains an Arabic official page for the medical-device QMS standard and identifies the adopted standard as **SFDA.MD/GSO ISO 13485:2017 / GSO ISO 13485:2016**. This gives the company a suitable Arabic official regulatory reference alongside the English ISO material. citeturn7search1\n\n### ISO 9001 status\n\nA significant timing issue emerged during the research: **ISO 9001:2015 is no longer the current edition**. ISO published **ISO 9001:2026** on 16 September 2026 and states that the 2015 edition has been withdrawn/replaced. Organizations certified to the old edition are directed to coordinate transition arrangements with their certification bodies. citeturn1search0turn1search4\n\nThe documents therefore do **not** present ISO 9001:2015 clause 8.7 as the current governing reference. They use **ISO 9001:2026** as a supplemental general-QMS reference while using ISO 13485:2016 for the medical-device-specific clause mapping. ISO itself distinguishes ISO 13485 from ISO 9001 on this basis: ISO 13485 is specifically designed around medical-device regulatory and safety requirements. citeturn1search0turn2view1\n\nISO also has an implementation handbook authored by ISO/TC 210 technical experts specifically to guide organizations in implementing and maintaining ISO 13485:2016, which supports treating ISO 13485 as the primary implementation reference here. citeturn5search1turn5search4\n\n## Sample record and label wording\n\n### Illustrative completed F-7-5-7-8\n\nUsing the supplied rejection-slip example as the basis, a clean illustrative completion would read:\n\n| Form field | Example entry |\n|---|---|\n| Department | Warehouse / OBM |\n| Date | 15 September 2026 |\n| Item Description | Feeding Tube |\n| Lot No. | 26051210 |\n| Unit | PCS |\n| Rejected Qty. | 1,350 |\n| Total Qty. | 1,350 |\n| Item Code | BU-111-0540 |\n| Reason for Rejection | All quantity identified as old stock; connector configuration changed and product is without graduation. |\n| Attachments | OBM inspection findings / product identification evidence |\n| Disposition | **Transfer to Warehouse for Destruction** |\n| Prepared by | QC Compliance / Quality Controller |\n| Production approval | N/A for Warehouse-origin product where the controlled form permits this |\n| Quality Manager | Signature and date required |\n| Factory Director | Signature and date required |\n| Any Note | “OBM reject documented on F-7-5-7-8. Product returned to Warehouse for approved destruction. Warehouse destruction evidence shall be attached before record closure.” |\n\nWhere the existing controlled F-7-5-7-8 does not yet contain a separate **Return to Vendor** checkbox, the temporary practical approach is to record **“Return to Vendor” under Other** until the form itself is formally revised. The SOP/WI should not silently redraw a controlled form without Document Control approval.\n\n### HOLD QC tag\n\nRecommended minimum label wording:\n\n```text\nHOLD QC\n\nDO NOT USE\nDO NOT MOVE\nDO NOT DESTROY WITHOUT QC DECISION\n\nItem / Product: __________________________\nItem Code: ______________________________\nLot / Batch: _____________________________\nQuantity: ________________________________\nSource: Assembly / Injection / Packaging /\n        Quarantine / OBM / Supplier\nReason: __________________________________\nF-7-5-7-8 No.: __________________ / N/A\nQC / Inspector: __________________________\nDate / Time: _____________________________\nLocation: ________________________________\n```\n\nAfter the formal decision has been signed, a useful warehouse status label is:\n\n```text\nREJECTED — APPROVED FOR DESTRUCTION\nTRANSFER TO WAREHOUSE ONLY\n\nItem Code: ______________________________\nLot / Batch: _____________________________\nQuantity: ________________________________\nF-7-5-7-8 No.: __________________________\nApproval Date: ___________________________\n```\n\nThe important distinction is that **HOLD QC is a pre-decision status**, while **Approved for Destruction is a post-approval status**. Combining them would weaken status control because personnel could not tell whether management authorization had actually been obtained.\n\n## Deliverables and final open items\n\nThe two requested English Word deliverables have been prepared as controlled-document-style final drafts.\n\n**Updated SOP — Reject Handling and Destruction Procedure**  \nIncludes the revised Daily Production logic, Quarantine requirements, mandatory OBM rejection slip, supplier-return assessment, HOLD QC controls, disposition branches, approval matrix, sample completed F-7-5-7-8, Mermaid source, process diagram, proposed timeline, and standards references.\n\n[Download the updated SOP — Word](sandbox:/mnt/data/Reject_Handling_and_Destruction_SOP_Final_Draft_EN.docx)\n\n**QC Work Instruction — Reject Handling, Approval and Disposition**  \nIncludes the operational QC sequence, quick decision table, form-completion instructions, Quarantine and OBM checklists, HOLD QC template, disposition execution instructions, approval matrix, sample form entry, flow diagram, Mermaid source, timeline and closure checks.\n\n[Download the QC Work Instruction — Word](sandbox:/mnt/data/WI_QC_Reject_Handling_Final_Draft_EN.docx)\n\nThe process diagram used in both documents is also available separately:\n\n[Download the decision-flow diagram](sandbox:/mnt/data/Reject_Handling_Decision_Flow.png)\n\nThree items remain deliberately identified rather than guessed. **Exactly 2.5% is still unspecified** by the source procedure and needs a formal management decision. fileciteturn0file0 The WI document number is left **TBD for Document Control** rather than inventing an uncontrolled identifier. Finally, the hour/day targets are marked as **proposed operating targets**, because the supplied company documents contain no approved response-time SLA.\n\nThe documents are therefore substantively finalized but labeled **FINAL DRAFT FOR APPROVAL** until Saudi Mais Document Control assigns/updates the controlled identifiers, effective date, revision approval and signatures."]},"status":"finished_successfully","end_turn":true,"weight":1.0,"metadata":{"finish_details":{"type":"stop","stop_tokens":[200002]},"is_complete":true,"content_references":[{"matched_text":"fileciteturn0file0","start_idx":654,"end_idx":675,"alt":null,"type":"file","name":"reject_Destruction_SOP.docx","id":"file_00000000136481fa842184b7d6340bf0","source":"my_files","snippet":null,"cloud_doc_url":null,"library_file_id":"libfile_bbbf87a3f8a08191bcf7323182e03849","library_artifact_type":null,"medical_file_reference":{"authors_display":null,"doi":null,"publication_year":null,"journal_name":null,"source_label":null,"journal_homepage_url":null,"source_url":null,"source_icon_url":null},"drug_file_reference":null,"page_range_start":null,"page_range_end":null,"input_pointer":{"message_index":0,"message_id":"89ecce92-6b3d-44fe-826c-12ef8a02c687","file_index":0,"line_range_start":null,"line_range_end":null},"fff_metadata":null,"connector_id":null},{"matched_text":"fileciteturn0file0","start_idx":1710,"end_idx":1731,"alt":null,"type":"file","name":"reject_Destruction_SOP.docx","id":"file_00000000136481fa842184b7d6340bf0","source":"my_files","snippet":null,"cloud_doc_url":null,"library_file_id":"libfile_bbbf87a3f8a08191bcf7323182e03849","library_artifact_type":null,"medical_file_reference":{"authors_display":null,"doi":null,"publication_year":null,"journal_name":null,"source_label":null,"journal_homepage_url":null,"source_url":null,"source_icon_url":null},"drug_file_reference":null,"page_range_start":null,"page_range_end":null,"input_pointer":{"message_index":0,"message_id":"89ecce92-6b3d-44fe-826c-12ef8a02c687","file_index":0,"line_range_start":null,"line_range_end":null},"fff_metadata":null,"connector_id":null},{"matched_text":"citeturn2view1turn5search16","prefix":null,"start_idx":2055,"end_idx":2086,"safe_urls":["https://committee.iso.org/standard/59752.html","https://www.iso.org/iso-